  "problem": "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,2).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$",
